    "raw_output": "### Provisional finding\n\nThe software provisionally falls within the scope of a high-risk AI system under Article 6(2) and Annex III, point 3, unless it satisfies the derogation criteria set out in Article 6(3) and the associated provider obligations under Article 6(4).\n\n### Why\n\nAnnex III, point 3(b) identifies as high-risk AI systems intended to be used to evaluate learning outcomes in educational and vocational training institutions at all levels. In addition, Annex III, point 3(c) covers AI systems intended to assess the appropriate level of education that an individual will receive or be able to access within such institutions. Scoring oral examinations evaluates learning outcomes, and assigning a Common European Framework of Reference (CEFR) level may also assess the appropriate educational level accessible to the student.\n\nUnder Article 6(2), systems listed in Annex III are considered high-risk by default. The fact that a teacher can alter the suggested CEFR level before it is delivered to the student does not automatically exempt the system. Under Article 6(3), an Annex III system is not considered high-risk only if it does not pose a significant risk of harm to health, safety, or fundamental rights, including by not materially influencing the outcome of decision-making, and meets at least one specific condition, such as performing a preparatory task to an assessment under Article 6(3)(d). If the software's score or suggested level materially influences the teacher\u2019s final determination, the derogation does not apply.\n\n### What could change this\n\n- The classification would not be high-risk if the system satisfies Article 6(3)\u2014for example, by acting strictly as a preparatory task under Article 6(3)(d) without materially influencing the teacher's independent decision-making.\n- To rely on an Article 6(3) derogation, the provider must satisfy Article 6(4), which requires documenting the assessment before the system is placed on the market or put into service and complying with registration requirements.\n- Conversely, pursuant to the final subparagraph of Article 6(3), if the system performs profiling of natural persons, it is always considered high-risk regardless of any other conditions.\n\n### Missing facts\n\n- The degree of teacher reliance on the automated score and suggested CEFR level (for example, whether teachers conduct a comprehensive, independent evaluation of the recording or routinely defer to the generated suggestion).\n- Whether the software performs profiling of students.\n- The precise institutional context and operational purpose of the test (such as formal academic credit, admission/placement, or purely informal practice).\n- Whether the provider evaluated and documented an assessment under Article 6(4) showing that the software does not materially influence the grading decision.\n\n### Timing and scope\n\nThis reading is provisional and confined to the classification subset of Article 6, Annex III, and Article 113. It does not assess general legal obligations, governance requirements, or overall regulatory conformity under the broader regulation. Under Article 113, the Regulation generally applies from 2 August 2026; however, under Article 113(c)(i), Chapter III, Sections 1, 2, and 3\u2014with the exception of Article 6(5)\u2014apply from 2 December 2027 for systems classified under Article 6(2) and Annex III.",
    "delivered_output": "### Provisional finding\n\nThe software provisionally falls within the scope of a high-risk AI system under Article 6(2) and Annex III, point 3, unless it satisfies the derogation criteria set out in Article 6(3) and the associated provider obligations under Article 6(4).\n\n### Why\n\nAnnex III, point 3(b) identifies as high-risk AI systems intended to be used to evaluate learning outcomes in educational and vocational training institutions at all levels. In addition, Annex III, point 3(c) covers AI systems intended to assess the appropriate level of education that an individual will receive or be able to access within such institutions. Scoring oral examinations evaluates learning outcomes, and assigning a Common European Framework of Reference (CEFR) level may also assess the appropriate educational level accessible to the student.\n\nUnder Article 6(2), systems listed in Annex III are considered high-risk by default. The fact that a teacher can alter the suggested CEFR level before it is delivered to the student does not automatically exempt the system. Under Article 6(3), an Annex III system is not considered high-risk only if it does not pose a significant risk of harm to health, safety, or fundamental rights, including by not materially influencing the outcome of decision-making, and meets at least one specific condition, such as performing a preparatory task to an assessment under Article 6(3)(d). If the software's score or suggested level materially influences the teacher\u2019s final determination, the derogation does not apply.\n\n### What could change this\n\n- The classification would not be high-risk if the system satisfies Article 6(3)\u2014for example, by acting strictly as a preparatory task under Article 6(3)(d) without materially influencing the teacher's independent decision-making.\n- To rely on an Article 6(3) derogation, the provider must satisfy Article 6(4), which requires documenting the assessment before the system is placed on the market or put into service and complying with registration requirements.\n- Conversely, pursuant to the final subparagraph of Article 6(3), if the system performs profiling of natural persons, it is always considered high-risk regardless of any other conditions.\n\n### Missing facts\n\n- The degree of teacher reliance on the automated score and suggested CEFR level (for example, whether teachers conduct a comprehensive, independent evaluation of the recording or routinely defer to the generated suggestion).\n- Whether the software performs profiling of students.\n- The precise institutional context and operational purpose of the test (such as formal academic credit, admission/placement, or purely informal practice).\n- Whether the provider evaluated and documented an assessment under Article 6(4) showing that the software does not materially influence the grading decision.\n\n### Timing and scope\n\nThis is a provisional reading against the pinned classification subset only: Article 6, Annex III, and Article 113, from the consolidated edition dated 27 July 2026. It does not cover the whole EU AI Act.\n\nFrom Article 113 \u2014 Entry into force and application:\n\n> It shall apply from 2 August 2026.\n\n> Chapter III, Sections 1, 2, and 3, with the exception of Article 6(5), shall apply from: (i) 2 December 2027 as regards AI systems classified as high-risk pursuant to Article 6(2) and Annex III; and (ii) 2 August 2028 as regards AI systems classified as high-risk pursuant to Article 6(1) and Annex I;\n"
